A||||14||LivoniaFieroGT||largest tire for the rear on a 86 GT||||10||
Z||000000||LivoniaFieroGT||09-08-2001||02:30 PM||||I am worn out the mouse clicking thru the archives.  Can anyone just answer swiftly, what is the biggest footprint and height (together) that can fit on an 86 GT? i.e. 235-60-15, 245...etc... I cannot have one mounted and test only to discard.  Local tire store salesmen only look dumb faced .... sorta like the oil mechanic when he goes to the front of a Fiero to change oil/fluids.||||reg||10
Z||000001||Cozmo||09-08-2001||03:24 PM||||On my '85 2M6 Targa I've got 215-65 rear and 205-65 front (15" diamond rims).  They fill the wheelwells better and provide a more "cushy" ride as well as give more ground clearance.  There is only a finger width between the rear tires and the spring, so I don't think you could go any wider there without some interference problems...<BR>Sure, you give up a little performance, but the car looks better with the bigger tires and it is more practical in the real world around potholes and speedbumps and such... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>BobC ||||reg||1
Z||000002||prtzlboy||09-08-2001||05:02 PM||||I've got 235 60 14 all the way around on my 85 GT||||reg||1
Z||000003||Formula88||09-08-2001||06:35 PM||||All depends on if you're talking on the factory rim or aftermarket.  If you're getting new rims, according to West Coast Fiero, you can fit 255/40-17s on a 17x9" rim with a 45mm offset.  This will clear all the suspension and fender and fit inside the fenderwell.  The 17" is necessary at 9" wide, cuz a 16x9 will hit the rear suspension.<P>For a factory rim, you're just limited to what will fit on the rim, mainly.  For a 15x7" rim, I'm thinking a 245/50-15 is about as big as you can go.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Standard||09-08-2001||07:14 PM||||225/60r15 is gonna be about as big as you want to go on a stock 15x7 rim.  Anything wider and there's way too much overhang which just leads to bad handling.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_pic_full.jpg">        <BR>1988 Quad 4 Coupe 5 speed<P><FONT SIZE="1">1986 GT 5 speed, 1984 SC Auto<BR><FONT COLOR="red">AOL: LastMinuteBastrd  AIM: SavedbyJebus</FONT></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000005||jimmybpei||09-08-2001||07:57 PM||||Thats Rite it reall depends on the size of the rim. i run 17" enkies and had a hard time with this as well, you really have to take into consideration the stock diameter and width. a 235 45 17 is close to the stock dia. but is way to wide. ended up looking like my 35" BF's off my 4x4. 205 40 17 is not tall enough and looks like donuts. There dosent seem to be any thing in between in a BF tire so i went with the new dual color <BR>SCORTHER 215 40 17 Think tey will fit all rite when they get here. oh ya the 235's stuck out way too far because it was about 2" wider than the stock tire<P>Hope this helps alittle <P>Thankx<BR>jimmy B<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||JohnF||09-08-2001||08:01 PM||||"...sorta like the oil mechanic when he goes to the front of a Fiero to change oil/fluids...."<P>I love it!  Same thing happens to me at the local GM dealer.  Kids in their Sophmore year are hired to change oil.  They have never SEEN a Fiero and ther eis a new one the next time you go! Hahahahah||||reg||1
Z||000007||2birds||09-08-2001||10:28 PM||||I have 225/60/15 rear and 215/60/15 fronts, on stock (15x7) rims.  With Susp Tech lowering springs, they are perfect, IMHO.  The fronts rub at full lock only when brand new.  The rears have plenty of clearance, and don't stick out or bulge too much.<P>For a pic, check it out on the N Florida Fiero site.  Mine's the red/silver 86 GT from SE Georgia<p>[This message has been edited by 2birds (edited 09-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||LivoniaFieroGT||09-09-2001||10:01 AM||||I am running stock rims.  I had hoped to fill the wheelwell a little more.  The car isn't used with handling in mind as this car is an automatic and slow...<BR>I was looking more for appearance and cruising economy.  Thanks for all your feedbacks.  I knew I'd get more info from Fiero experts than a clown at the tire store.<BR>Rob||||reg||1
Z||000009||samoht||09-09-2001||04:22 PM||||I am a clown at a tire staore, just be carefully you dont through your speedo off to much, as for going wide you are only talking about 30mm differance.. not that big of a deal.. I would look for a tire that looks wider.. So have flatter tread then other, like Dunlop ..  But it goes up in price.. you wont find to much in a 14.. good luck||||reg||1
Z||000010||Joe Torma||09-09-2001||05:58 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by jimmybpei:<BR><B>i run 17" enkies and had a hard time with this as well, you really have to take into consideration the stock diameter and width. a 235 45 17 is close to the stock dia. but is way to wide</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Jimmy,<BR>Do you have a picture of that size on your 17s?  I'm thinking about that size.  How wide are your rims?  Mine are 7.25.  I have 225/45s right now.<P>Thanks<P><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/simplesig.gif">  <BR>Did you get your car in my book yet????<BR> <A HREF="http://www.animatedconcepts.com/fierobook.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.animatedconcepts.com/fierobook.htm</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Alex4mula||09-09-2001||08:56 PM||||I don't know about pre 88 cars but I have 235-40-17 on my 17" rims in my Formula and by no means they are way to wide. They fit perfectly and look great. On the other hand I have seen 235-45-17 and I think they bulge too much and look bad. Maybe with 18" rims and 35 profile you can get by with 245 on 8"-9' rims.<BR>I have used 225-50-15 in my old (15") rims but the diameter was a little short. 225-55-15 or 235-50-15 may fit better if you can find them.<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||artherd||09-10-2001||01:09 AM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/northstar/17-rear.jpg"> <P>17x7 215/45/17 Dunlop Sp Sport W10s all around.<P>Handles like a dream, I LOVE MY 17s!! (Trav rules  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000013||SwissFormula||09-10-2001||06:16 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by artherd:<BR><B>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/northstar/17-rear.jpg">  <P>17x7 215/45/17 Dunlop Sp Sport W10s all around.<P>Handles like a dream, I LOVE MY 17s!! (Trav rules   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hi Artherd<P>I want to put 17x7's on my Formula too, but i fear that the front wheels will stick out. Do they fit with an offset of 40mm without to stick out?<BR>Ask because thats illegal where i live...<P>ThanX<BR>Roy<P>||||reg||5
Z||000014||SwissFormula||09-10-2001||07:24 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by artherd:<BR><B>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/northstar/17-rear.jpg">  <P>17x7 215/45/17 Dunlop Sp Sport W10s all around.<P>Handles like a dream, I LOVE MY 17s!! (Trav rules   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hi Artherd<P>I want to put 17x7's on my Formula too, but i fear that the front wheels will stick out. Do they fit with an offset of 40mm without to stick out?<BR>Ask because thats illegal where i live...<P>ThanX<BR>Roy<P>||||reg||5
